### Anything else relevant?

**The canonical application-side mitigation does not stop it.** A `returnUrl` check requiring a relative, non-protocol-relative path accepts `/.;/(//evil.test)`, so an application that validated correctly still redirects off-origin:

```ts
export const returnUrlGuard = (route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot) => {
  const target = route.queryParamMap.get("returnUrl") ?? "/";
  if (!target.startsWith("/") || target.startsWith("//")) {
    return true;
  }
  return inject(Router).parseUrl(target);
};
```

With `{ path: "login", component: Login, canActivate: [returnUrlGuard] }` added to the config above:

```text
/login?returnUrl=%2F.%3B%2F(%2F%2Fevil.test)   302  location: //evil.test   <- accepted
/login?returnUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fevil.test       200  no redirect             <- rejected
/login?returnUrl=%2F%2Fevil.test               200  no redirect             <- rejected
```

`router.navigateByUrl(target)` behaves the same. This moves the payload into a query parameter, so the delivered link is `/login?returnUrl=...` rather than a visibly odd path. `search` is concatenated unchanged, so query parameters reach the redirect target.

**Also reachable without a literal `.` or `//` in the request:** `GET /%2e;/(/\evil.test)` returns `location: //evil.test`, since `%2e` is decoded and `\` normalized after any inspection of the raw path.

**Affected shapes:** a parameter route with children, plain or lazy, whose descendants can match a two-segment group, at URL depth ≤ 2. Childless parameter routes, children without a `**`, a `**` child using `redirectTo`.

