## Evidence

`server-spi-private/src/main/java/org/keycloak/policy/DenylistPasswordPolicyProviderFactory.java:271`
builds precomputed entries with `Locale.ROOT`, while
`server-spi-private/src/main/java/org/keycloak/policy/DenylistPasswordPolicyProvider.java:48`
uses parameterless `toLowerCase()`. This is distinct from intended case folding: the
two sides of the same lookup apply different functions.

## Suggested fix

Use the default locale for all denylist normalization paths, so that it matches the existing, non-precomputed code path that existed for a long time.

## Describe the bug

The `DefaultCredentialOfferProvider` authorizes cross-user offer creation by comparing only role names across all direct user role mappings — both realm and client roles. A client role named `credential-offer-create` satisfies the check intended for Keycloak's dedicated realm role, because the comparison uses unqualified string matching instead of role identity.

With the pre-authorized-code feature enabled, the resulting offer creates an access token for the victim user on the attacker's OID4VCI client, enabling issuance of a victim-subject credential bound to the attacker's holder key.

This issue applies only when the `oid4vc-vci-rest-credential-offer` feature is enabled.

## Version

`main`

## Expected behavior

Only users with the dedicated **realm** role `credential-offer-create` can create cross-user credential offers. The authorization check resolves the specific realm role and uses `hasRole()` identity comparison. Client roles with the same name do not satisfy this check.

## Actual behavior

The authorization check at `DefaultCredentialOfferProvider` lines 167-172 streams all direct role mappings (realm and client) and compares by name string only. Any client role named `credential-offer-create` passes the check, allowing users without the realm role to create targeted offers for other users.

